nodecode-parcel is a rewrite of promptclickrun's promptclickrun/hermes-parcel:

  https://github.com/promptclickrun/hermes-parcel
  commit 9641b4092f1373ca021285e80b0885cb9e1c7fa3

Apache-2.0, Copyright (c) 2026 promptclickrun (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the whole plugin. The original is a Python Hermes Agent
plugin: a client for Parcel's external API on the standard library, three
model tools (parcel_deliveries, parcel_add_delivery, parcel_carriers), a
/parcel slash command and a bundled skill. This cell is Common Lisp: the same
three tools, the same /parcel command, the same three endpoints with the same
query and the same JSON body, the same status numbers turned into the same
words, the same compact record a model reads (description, tracking number,
carrier, status, expected dates, latest event or the whole history), the same
carrier lookup, and the same local guards (a ten-minute cache, a local count
of Parcel's 20 reads an hour and 20 adds a day, the last list answered marked
stale when the limit is spent, and an add checked before it is sent). The
config keys carry over as api_base, api_key and api_key_env, and the request
timeouts, the cache age and both limits are settings here too. Requests go
through the image's own HTTP call; nothing is fetched at run time and there
is no Python.

What was left out: the skill file, whose doctrine (read once, answer from that
result, lead with what needs the operator, ask before adding) now lives in the
cell's help topic and tool descriptions; and the on-disk state file, so the
cache and the request counts live in the running process and a restart costs
at most one read. The original refused HTTP redirects outright so the API key
could not be forwarded; the image's own HTTP call follows up to five, which
is its behaviour and not this cell's to change.
